    'app.note.sobel': 'Sobel: Gx and Gy with weights ±1 and ±2 (the same operations as the unrolled MiniC code), and the output is (|Gx| + |Gy|) / 4. The positive and negative parts of each gradient are accumulated with the unit and subtracted exactly. With an adder, the sums are approximate; with a multiplier, the products by 1 and by 2, which have a single partial product per row: as in the 1 2 1 Gaussian, several techniques (approximate compressors, Mitchell) make no error at all here, and only truncation and DRUM show up. The error shows up on the edges, where the gradient is large, and as scattered noise in flat regions.',
    'app.note.sharpen': 'Enhancement: 5 times the pixel minus the 4 neighbors, clamped to 0 and 255. With a multiplier, the product by 5 has two partial products per row; with an adder, the sum of the neighbors is approximate. Since the result is a difference, a small error in one of the parts becomes a large error relative to the final value in flat regions.',
