    'hls.ap.reason.sub': 'subtraction with a result wider than the unit: exact only',
    'hls.ap.reason.signed': 'signed, result wider than the unit: exact only',
    'hls.ap.outNote': 'Each cell shows the value estimated by the static model and the value measured in the bit accurate run with the same inputs, only at the positions the kernel writes. MED, WCE, EP and MRED are measured; SSIM (11 × 11 Gaussian window) is estimated from the error of each output value band.',
    'hls.ap.single': 'For a single position output, the measured value is the error of that run (the measured MSE is its square) and the estimate includes the variance over inputs.',
    'hls.ap.img.exact': '{a} exact',
    'hls.ap.img.approx': '{a} approximate',
    'hls.ap.img.diff': 'difference (× 8)',
    'hls.ap.modelTitle': 'Error model and verification',
    'hls.ap.modelHelp': '<p>The error of a value is the approximate value minus the exact one. The static model keeps the mean (bias) and the second moment of the error of each node and propagates them from the approximate units to the outputs, without running the approximate kernel. Signal statistics come from one exact run with the chosen input data (the profile): mean and second moment of each value, 4096 samples of the operands of each operation and 2048 sampled executions of each segment.</p><ul><li>Own error: the error of the unit over the operand samples of that operation (a multiplier errs differently with coefficient 19 and with 52).</li><li>Analytic: addition, e = ea ± eb + eu; product, e = a·eb + b·ea + ea·eb + eu; right shift with its rounding (E[p(1 − p)], p = (e mod 2^k) / 2^k); masks; comparison and selection by the probability that the decision flips. Errors are assumed independent of each other and of the signals.</li><li>Sampled: inside each segment, the sampled executions are replayed with the approximate units, and the error that arrives from other segments, recurrences and memory enters as normal noise with the propagated mean and variance. Correlations inside the segment (an adder that already cleared the low bits, an error that depends on the low bits of the operands) are kept.</li><li>Recurrence: the accumulator error evolves as e(t + 1) = g·e(t) + d, with the gain g (analytic) and the increment d measured on the body; inside the loop the mean over iterations applies, after it the final value. Truncated or segmented adders in an accumulator are estimated as if the accumulator had arbitrary low bits, which overestimates the error of truncation.</li><li>Memory: each array keeps the mean of the errors written to it, weighted by executions.</li></ul><p>The verification runs the elaborated design (the same loops, segments, truncations and memory widths as the hardware) with the functional models of the units: the exhaustive table for 8 bit multipliers, the netlist for 16 bit ones and the arithmetic model, checked by the tests, for adders. Signed additions and subtractions use the same units in two’s complement, modulo 2^W: a signed addition is the sum of the W bit codes, and a subtraction is computed as ~(~a + b), which needs no carry in (the inversions are wires on FPGA and inverters on ASIC, which the model does not count). Only when the result fits the W bits of the unit; otherwise the operation stays exact. Unsigned multipliers receive the magnitudes and the sign is applied afterwards; Booth only appears when the operands fit the signed range.</p><p>Area, energy and delay in the report tab already use the chosen units: exact and approximate units of the same width are different resources in scheduling and binding.</p>',
