    'dse.helpTitle': 'How the exploration works',
    'dse.help': '<p>Each point is an assignment of units to the additions, subtractions and multiplications, with the directives of the code and the period of the editor. The point is synthesized again, so the cost includes unit sharing, multiplexers, registers and the II; the error of each output comes from the static model of the Approximation tab.</p><ul><li><b>Groups</b>: <em>per operation</em>, each DFG operation, after unrolling, picks its own unit (in the Gaussian, the 9 multiplications of the accumulator line can have different units, and coefficient 52 tolerates less error than 19); <em>per source line</em>, all operations of the same line, class and width get the same unit, as a designer would annotate by hand. Per operation saves more; per line, the space is smaller and the result easier to read.</li><li><b>Candidates</b>: in each group, only the units on the Pareto front between the saving in context (the whole design synthesized with only that group changed, not the isolated unit in the table) and the own error on the operations of the group, measured over the real operands. Units that save nothing alone and units that never err on any sample are dropped: with the same results on the real operands, synthesis turns them into the same circuit as the exact one.</li><li><b>Local search</b> (default): starts from each of the starting points (the result of efficiency and the optimum with one unit per operation class, carried to the groups) and applies, in cost order, the first move of a group that saves and keeps the constraint; with none, pairs: a move that saves but violates, with another that gives error back in another group. It never ends worse than any starting point.</li><li><b>Efficiency</b>: from the exact design, at each step it applies the swap with the largest cost saving per error increase that keeps the constraint, until no swap saves. Fast, but it stops at local optima.</li><li><b>Sequential</b>: groups in source order, multiplications before additions; in each, the cheapest candidate that keeps the constraint.</li><li><b>Tabu</b>: from the same starting points, at each iteration it moves to the best feasible neighbor outside the tabu list, even if more expensive than the current one; it ends with the local search.</li><li><b>GRASP</b>: randomized greedy constructions, each followed by the local search.</li><li><b>Genetic</b>: one gene per group, tournament with feasibility rules, uniform crossover, mutation and elitism; it ends with the local search. Fixed seed: results repeat.</li></ul><p>In the studies (E2), local search finds the best known point in 11 of 12 per operation configurations; tabu, GRASP and the genetic algorithm stay close to it, with no gain on the example kernels. The result of each heuristic is the cheapest feasible point among those it evaluated; it is executed bit by bit and, if the measured error exceeds the bound, the next feasible point in cost order is verified. The step by step view shows each iteration. The constraint holds for every output (SSIM for image outputs: estimated in the search and measured in the verification); weighted adds area, energy and latency divided by those of the exact design. <b>Download</b> takes the point to the Approximation tab and downloads the project for Vitis HLS and Vivado. Exhaustive, random, per class groups and a list of periods remain in the command line, for the studies.</p>',
